
✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅ Connection Pooling ✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅

Creating a DB connection is expensive:
TCP connection setup
Authentication
Session initialization
Memory allocation on DB

Opening a new connection for every request adds latency and increases DB load.

Databases also have limited connections:
PostgreSQL default ≈ 100
MySQL default ≈ 151

During traffic spikes, applications can exhaust DB connections and cause failures.

Connection Pooling
Instead of creating new connections repeatedly, applications reuse existing DB connections.

Flow:
Server asks pool for a connection
Pool provides an idle connection
Query executes
Connection returns to pool instead of closing

This avoids repeated connection setup overhead and improves performance.

Types
Internal Pooling
Pooling inside application instances.
Problem:
3 app instances × 150 pool size = 450 DB connections

Scaling app servers increases total DB connections rapidly.

External Pooling
Use a dedicated pooler like PgBouncer.
App Servers -> PgBouncer -> Database
All app instances share a centralized pool, reducing DB connection pressure and handling spikes more efficiently.

const mysql = require("mysql2/promise");
const pool = mysql.createPool({
  host: "localhost",
  user: "root",
  password: "password",
  database: "app_db",

  waitForConnections: true,

  // maxConnections
  connectionLimit: 100,

  // maxIdle
  maxIdle: 10,

  // idleTimeout
  idleTimeout: 30000,

  // connectionTimeout
  connectTimeout: 5000
});

connectionLimit (maxConnections)
Maximum DB connections pool can create.
connectionLimit: 100

If all are busy:
requests wait
or fail after timeout

Too high → DB overload
Too low → request queueing

maxIdle
Maximum idle connections kept open.
maxIdle: 10
Reuses existing connections instead of reopening TCP/auth/session repeatedly.

Too high → wasted DB resources
Too low → reconnect overhead

idleTimeout
How long idle connections stay open.
idleTimeout: 30000
Unused connections are closed after 30s.

connectTimeout
Maximum wait time while establishing DB connection.
connectTimeout: 5000
Prevents requests hanging forever during DB/network issues.

✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅ Database Scaling ✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅
Read Replicas
Primary DB -> Read Replicas (India, US, Europe)

Write queries go to primary DB.
Read queries are distributed to replicas.

Benefits
Reduces load on primary DB
Lower read latency for global users
Improves read scalability

In most applications:
~70% of traffic is read-heavy
So replicas significantly reduce database pressure.

Replication Lag Problem
Replication is asynchronous in many databases.

Example:
User updates profile name → write goes to primary DB
User immediately refreshes profile
Read request goes to replica
Replica is delayed by 200ms
User sees stale data

This is called:
Replication Lag
For payments, invoices, balances, or order status, stale reads can create major consistency problems.

======================== Handling Consistency ========================
1. Read-After-Write Consistency
After a user performs a write:
temporarily route related reads to primary DB
avoid replicas until replication catches up

Example:
Update profile -> next profile read goes to primary DB
Very common strategy in production systems.

2. Replication Lag Tracking
Track replica lag and avoid serving reads from delayed replicas.
If lag exceeds threshold:
Replica lag > 500ms -> stop routing reads
Some systems block reads until replication completes.

Tradeoff:
better consistency
higher latency

======================== Sharding ========================

As tables grow to billions of rows, a single database instance becomes difficult to scale.
orders table -> billions of rows

Problems:
Slow queries
Large indexes
Expensive joins
Storage limits
High write pressure

Sharding
Splitting data across multiple database instances.
Shard 1 -> Orders 1 to 5 billion
Shard 2 -> Orders 5 to 10 billion

The biggest challenge:
How do you decide which data goes to which shard?

This is called:
shard key selection

Common shard keys:
user_id
region
order_id hash

What Problems Does Sharding Solve?
1. Query Scalability
Smaller datasets per shard:
smaller indexes
fewer scanned rows

2. Write Scalability
Writes get distributed across multiple DB instances instead of one primary DB handling everything.
Modern Distributed Databases

Some databases handle:
sharding
replication
failover
distributed transactions

Examples:
PlanetScale
Neon
CockroachDB
